    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how a capacitor works in an ac circuit compared to dc? specifically why does it block dc but let ac through? also what is the formula for capacitive reactance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor acts as an open circuit once fully charged because the constant voltage prevents any further current flow, effectively blocking direct current. Conversely, in an AC circuit, the continuously changing voltage forces the charge to repeatedly build up and discharge, allowing an alternating current to pass through the component. The opposition a capacitor offers to AC is called capacitive reactance, which is calculated using the formula $X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi f C}$, where $f$ is the frequency and $C$ is the capacitance. This means the capacitor blocks low-frequency or DC signals well but allows higher-frequency AC signals to pass with less resistance.",
      "source": "target",
