      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right; there is a time delay because the electrons need time to move, similar to collecting sand into a bucket before spilling it. At the microscopic level, the capacitor is not a conductor because the metal plates are electrically isolated by a dielectric material (like ceramic or air) that prevents electrons from jumping across. Instead, an electric field builds up inside that gap against the flow of electrons, creating that \"friction\" or opposition known as reactance. This effect becomes more pronounced at lower frequencies where the voltage changes slowly, giving the particles more time to establish a stable charge block before the signal shifts.",
